Regulatory Environment
Our interstate natural gas transmission systems and storage operations are regulated by the FERC under the Natural Gas Act of 1938, the Natural Gas Policy Act of 1978 and the Energy Policy Act of 2005. We operate under tariffs approved by the FERC that establish rates, cost recovery mechanisms and other terms and conditions of services to our customers. The rates established under our tariffs are a function of our costs of providing services to our customers, including a reasonable return on our invested capital.
Generally, the FERC’s authority also extends to:
• rates and charges for natural gas transportation and storage;
• certification and construction of new facilities;
• extension or abandonment of services and facilities;
• maintenance of accounts and records;
• relationships between pipelines and certain affiliates;
• terms and conditions of service;
• depreciation and amortization policies;
